.TH XCB 1 "2026-11-08" "notcoreutils" "notcoreutils Manual"
.SH NAME
xcb \- clipboard manager with history
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xcb copy
[\fITEXT\fR...]
.br
.B xcb paste
.br
.B xcb list
.br
.B xcb select
\fIN\fR
.br
.B xcb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xcb
is a real clipboard manager: it owns the X11 CLIPBOARD selection
(the same ICCCM pattern proven in this codebase's own
.BR st (1)
clipboard patch \- XSetSelectionOwner/SelectionRequest/
XConvertSelection), keeps a RAM ring buffer of history (not just the
single most recent entry), and is reachable from anywhere: plain
CLI, an interactive TUI, and any real GUI app on the system (since it
is a real CLIPBOARD owner, Ctrl+V in a browser or GUI text field
works exactly as it would against any other clipboard manager).
.PP
Daemon + client, one binary: the first
.BR copy / paste /TUI
invocation auto\-starts a background daemon (an X11 connection plus a
Unix socket at
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xcb.sock ,
or
.I /tmp/.xcb-<uid>.sock
if that is unset) if one is not already running; every later
invocation is just a client talking to it over the socket. The
daemon holds all clipboard state in RAM (a fixed\-size static ring
buffer, no dynamic allocation, no disk\-backed history file) for as
long as it is alive.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B copy \fR[\fITEXT\fR...]
Copy TEXT (or stdin, if no TEXT is given) onto the clipboard.
.TP
.B paste
Print the current clipboard contents.
.TP
.B list
Print the full history, most recent first.
.TP
.B select \fIN\fR
Re\-activate history entry N as the current clipboard contents.
.TP
.B (no args)
Launch an interactive TUI: fuzzy\-search history, Enter to
re\-activate an old entry.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wlcb (1),
.BR st (1)
